{
  "id": "Compound::DB00869 - Disease::DOID:1686",
  "verbalization": "Dorzolamide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Glaucoma through a series of interconnected mechanisms involving the treatment of myopia and eyelid pain. The evidence suggests that Dorzolamide may act as a causal agent in the development of Myopia Transient or Eyelid Pain, which in turn are linked to Glaucoma through indirect pathways. Both Myopia Transient and Eyelid Pain are treated by Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors and Organic Heterocyclic Compounds, which are shared as common ancestors in the network. This suggests a potential role for Dorzolamide in the broader context of ocular health, possibly through its effects on fluid balance or inflammation, which could contribute to the development of Glaucoma. The shared biological pathways and the presence of common ancestors highlight a possible link between Dorzolamide and Glaucoma, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
